SNMP MIB for monitoring the health of the Greenplum Database (3.x)
------------------------------------------------------------------
6/30/2008   Ben Werther    bwerther@greenplum.com
-------------------------------------------------

NOTE: This is for internal Greenplum use only, or as a package installed by authorized Greenplum personel on customer systems. The source code should not be given to customers at this point in time.

I've adapted and extended the basic Postgres SNMP MIB to support health monitoring of a Greenplum array.

The Postgres MIB implements the standard RDBMS MIB, which provides general vendor neutral information about the configuration of a database.

For details of the underlying MIB, see http://publib.boulder.ibm.com/infocenter/idshelp/v10/index.jsp?topic=/com.ibm.snmp.doc/snmp80.htm or the actual MIB file enclosed.


How it operates:
- The mode in which I've been using this package is as a standalone SNMP agent. It needs net-snmp to be on the system to build, but runs independently on its own port.

Building the MIB:
- Requires that the build box has a GPDB or Postgres tree, and has net-snmp installed.
- Copy this folder into the contrib folder of GPDB or Postgres
- make clean; make
- Let me know if you have any problems building

Installing the MIB:
- Copy the APPLICATION-MIB and RDBMS-MIB files to the /usr/share/snmp/mibs folder on the target machine
- Put pgsnmpd and pgsnmpd.conf in a convenient directory 
- Edit the agentaddress setting in pgsnmpd.conf to set the UDP port that it will listen on (currently set to 160)
- Run pgsnmpd with syntax like: pgsnmpd -c pgsnmpd.conf -C "host=127.0.0.1 user=gpadmin dbname=pgsnmpd" where -C is the connect string to the Greenplum database
- From an snmp client you should be able to browse this MIB

    snmpwalk -c public localhost:160 -v2c RDBMS-MIB::rdbmsRelState       will give you the state of the system and the segments --

         [root@localhost pgsnmpd-1.0]# snmpwalk -c public localhost:160 -v2c RDBMS-MIB::rdbmsRelState
          RDBMS-MIB::rdbmsRelState.1.1 = INTEGER: active(2)
          RDBMS-MIB::rdbmsRelState.2.0 = INTEGER: active(2)
          RDBMS-MIB::rdbmsRelState.2.1 = INTEGER: active(2)
          RDBMS-MIB::rdbmsRelState.2.2 = INTEGER: active(2)
          RDBMS-MIB::rdbmsRelState.2.3 = INTEGER: active(2)

  rdbmsRelState.1.1 is the entire array, and can have the value:
     active(2) -- if it is queryable
     unavailable(5) -- if the array or a required segement is down

     [it determines this by connecting to the array and doing a tiny distributed query every 60 seconds]

  rdbmsRelState.2.x are the segnents, and can have the values:
    active(2) -- currently valid and acting as a primary
    available(3) -- valid but not acting as a primary (i.e. a standby)
    unavailable(4) -- not valid
    other(1) -- anything else (this is not expected to occur)
     
    [it determines these from the gp_configuration table]



 Good luck, and let me know if you have any questions...

   -Ben



